#!/usr/bin/env bash
#MISE description="PostToolUse hook body: release the hold when the handoff tool completes, because a human answering ExitPlanMode or AskUserQuestion produces a tool result and not a prompt"
#
# CLOUD-485. `plan-hold-release`'s sibling, and the half CLOUD-451 was missing.
#
# **The guard and the release were listening to different things.**
# `plan-hold-guard` gates `PreToolUse` on `ExitPlanMode|AskUserQuestion`, while
# `plan-hold-release` is wired to `UserPromptSubmit`. Those are not the same
# event: a human answering either of those two tools produces a **tool result**,
# not a prompt. So for the exact case the mechanism exists to serve, the answer
# was invisible to the release, and the hold ran on until its cap or until
# somebody removed the sentinel by hand.
#
# Measured 2026-08-12: a hold armed, an `AskUserQuestion` answered, the hold
# still live afterwards. The prompt path was never broken — a typed reply
# released a hold correctly in the same session — which is why the fix adds an
# event rather than changing `plan-hold-release`.
#
# **No classifier here, and that is the point rather than an omission.**
# `plan-hold-release` must decide whether a prompt came from a person, because
# `UserPromptSubmit` carries machine turns too — notifications, webhook
# deliveries — and releasing on one of those drops the door while somebody is
# mid-sentence. Here the provenance is structural: this event fires only after
# one of two tools whose entire purpose is to ask a human, and the tool
# completing IS the human having answered. There is no prose to shape-test, so
# there is no shape test to get wrong.
#
# **The `tool_name` check is belt to the matcher's braces.** The settings entry
# already scopes this to the two handoff tools, so the case below is redundant
# today. It is here because the failure it prevents is silent and expensive: a
# matcher widened later — or this body reused from another entry — would turn
# every tool call into a release, which is the reclaim CLOUD-451 exists to
# prevent, arriving through the file meant to fix it.
#
# **What it must NOT do is release on a turn merely ending.** A handoff turn ends
# idle on purpose and the hold must outlive it; that is the whole mechanism. Only
# the answer releases. `tests/plan-hold.bats` asserts the non-handoff payload
# leaves the sentinel standing, because that is the direction a future
# "simplification" would break without any test going red.
#
# **Cost.** CLOUD-435 deleted the per-Bash-call `PreToolUse` entries over ~150 ms
# of task-runner startup each. That argument does not reach this one, for the
# same reason it does not reach `plan-hold-guard`: `ExitPlanMode` and
# `AskUserQuestion` fire at most once per turn, the frequency class of the `Stop`
# and `UserPromptSubmit` entries it kept. Invoked by path, never `mise run`, so
# no task-runner startup is paid at all.
#
# Silent and always 0, like its sibling. A hook on the tool path has no verdict
# to deliver, and an agent does not need telling that a sentinel it never sees
# was removed.
set -uo pipefail

raw=$(cat 2>/dev/null) || exit 0

tool=$(printf '%s' "$raw" | jq -r '.tool_name // empty' 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
case "$tool" in
ExitPlanMode | AskUserQuestion) ;;
*) exit 0 ;;
esac

# The hold directory is spelled in `plan-hold-check` and nowhere else, the
# single-spelling discipline `tests/plan-hold.bats` already pins — this file
# asks for it rather than re-deriving `$GIT_DIR/batten-holds`.
check="$(dirname -- "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/plan-hold-check"
[ -x "$check" ] || exit 0

dir=$("$check" dir 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
[ -n "$dir" ] && [ -d "$dir" ] || exit 0

# Release, never signal — removing the sentinel lets the sleeper finish on its
# own terms and return 0, and that exit is the wake-up the harness delivers with
# the human's answer already in hand. Killing it would end the process without
# that exit, which is the "the work runs and the session loses its wake-up"
# failure `run-shape-guard` catalogues. This file sends no signal at all, so the
# property holds structurally rather than by review.
for file in "$dir"/*; do
	[ -f "$file" ] || continue
	rm -f -- "$file" 2>/dev/null || true
done

exit 0
